>> title: Bánh Mì Đặc Biệt
>> category: Sandwiches & Rolls
>> tags: pork, vietnamese, sandwich, cold cuts, no cook
>> counters: Phở & Bánh Mì
>> aka: banh mi dac biet, bánh mì đặc biệt, dac biet, đặc biệt, the combo one, combo banh mi, combination banh mi, house special banh mi, special banh mi, banh mi thit nguoi, Vietnamese sandwich, number 1, S1
>> pairs-with: banh-mi-khong, pork-liver-pate, do-chua, cha-lua, mayonnaise
>> servings: 4
>> time: 15 min
>> step.1: crisp 4 min, split along one edge
>> step.2: spread both cheeks
>> step.3: layer, close, press

Crisp @bánh mì rolls{4} in a 400°F (200°C) #oven{} ~bake{4%min}, then split each one along a single edge so it stays hinged, and pull a little crumb out of the top half to make room.

Spread @&(~1)split rolls{} with @unsalted butter{2%Tbs}(30 g; soft) on the top cheek, and @mayonnaise{1/4%cup}(60 mL) and @pork liver pâté{1/3%cup}(80 g) on the bottom. The sandwich's mayonnaise is not quite the Deli's: whole egg plus an extra yolk, so it is looser, paler and a little sweet, and it goes on thick rather than smeared.

Layer @&(~1)dressed rolls{} with @chả lụa{8%oz}(225 g; sliced paper-thin), @thịt nguội{4%oz}(115 g; any sliced pork cold cut), @đồ chua{1%cup}(drained hard), @cucumber{1/2}(in long batons), @cilantro{1%cup}(whole sprigs, stems on) and @bird chiles{2}(sliced), shake @Maggi seasoning{1%tsp} down the middle, then close it and press once with the heel of your hand. Đặc biệt means the special — whatever the case holds that morning — so the three cold cuts above are one shop's answer to a rule rather than the rule itself. Swap in giò thủ, chả chiên or head cheese and it is still the number one.
